alk is the  average of these two cases this ends up  being 2 plus 1/2 square root of 2 which  is approximately 2.7 1  can you do better than this the answer  is yes the solution is you need to  consider walking something in between  these two extreme cases imagine you walk  a distance X above the halfway point now  if there is no troll you can simply  complete your trip to point B if there  is a troll you need to walk around the  barrier but you've already walked some  distance X up so that'll save you the  amount you'll need to walk around the  barrier so let's calculate the distances  in both cases 50% of the day there is no  troll so you can simply walk to point B  the distance you walk is the hypotenuse  this is the square root of 1 plus x  squared and need to walk two of these in  the other 50% of the cases there is a  troll and you're going to need to walk  around this barrier so you walk this  distance up X then you walk around and  then you complete your trip so the first  distance is the same hypotenuse now  instead of having to walk a full  distance one up you can walk 1 minus X  up and then you complete your square  root of 2 journey to the end so the  total distance you walk is the sum of  these distances the expected distance  will be the average of these two cases  so we'll average these two distances and  we'll get an expected distance that you  walk in terms of the function X we want  to minimize this distance so you could  take the derivative with respect to X  and solve for the minimum value after  setting the derivative equal to 0 this  ends up being X is equal to  approximately 0.35 we substitute that  back into our distance function and we  get that you expect to walk about 2.6 2  miles so that's one way to solve this  problem I'm going to present another way  so from the start you can't really know  which distance you're going to walk up  but you could have a compass and you  could say well I'm going to walk some  angle up so imagine we walk so